WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:03.369
GRADY POWELL: John, Michael,
gentlemen, congratulations.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:04.771
The two of you have made it
through the first two rounds

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:05.971
of our competition
and are moving forward

00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:07.273
into the final
round, where you're

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.741
heading back to your
home forges to build

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:10.276
an iconic weapon from history.

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:13.078
The is the the largest blade
we have ever asked bladesmiths

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:15.314
to make in the final round.

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:19.352
Gentlemen, in four days,
you will have to build this.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:25.858
[sighing]

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:29.028
The Grote Pier sword.

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.596
NARRATOR: During
the 16th century,

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.899
a larger-than-life warrior
in the Netherlands known

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:35.935
to the locals as Grote
Pier, or the Giant Pier,

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:38.270
wielded this massive
sword in battle.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.240
Pier's size made him the
subject of folklore and legend.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.444
But the physical size of
his sword speaks for itself.

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:47.379
The 15-pound, nearly
7-foot tall sword

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:50.517
is the largest European sword
ever to be used in combat.

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.518
As a mercenary and
pirate, Grote Pier

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:54.687
is believed to have wielded
this weapon with ease,

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:58.324
delivering devastating blows to
any enemy that got in his way.

00:00:58.424 --> 00:01:00.092
And it can be seen
today at the Frieze

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:01.461
Museum in the Netherlands.

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:03.129
GRADY POWELL: Now,
guys, good luck.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:05.598
You have four days to
complete your massive swords.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.034 line:14%
We will see you then.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.670 line:14%
[music playing]

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.305 line:8%
JOHN: My name is John
Simons from Indianapolis.

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:15.241 line:8%
I have a three
years bladesmithing.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:17.577 line:14%
Most of that's all self-taught.

00:01:17.643 --> 00:01:18.878
I love doing this.

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.513
Some people want to
go to Disney World.

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:21.848
I want to come here.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:23.716
This is my vacation, so I'm
really looking forward to it.

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:27.687
This blade is so long, I can
only heat up about eight inches

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.856
at a time, press it.

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:31.757
Just a very repetitive process.

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:34.760
Prepping for this quench is
going to take a little time.

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:36.262
I have three forges.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:40.032
I can get nearly all that
sword in the forges at once.

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:42.101
This is a super-sized
quench tank.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:43.369
Never used it yet.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.271
We're going to
test it out today.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.206
Quenching this-- I've
never had to climb

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.841
five rungs of a ladder.

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:52.778
Everything needs to go right
because there's not enough time

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:54.614
to do it all over again.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.316
Hope for the best.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.952
I didn't feel anything
abnormal when it quenched.

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:01.187
It is hard.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:02.889
And most importantly,
it is straight.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:04.490
All the components are there.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.860
They just need some finishing
touches and put an edge on it.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.030
The blade, it is 100% complete.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:14.033
I'm exhausted, but inside,
it feels wonderful.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.502
The mother of all swords.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:22.775 line:8%
MICHAEL: My name is Michael
Dillon, 54 years old.

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:25.211
Been forging for 30 years.

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:29.749
I started making knives for
props in the movie industry.

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:32.685
I'm pretty confident but
worried my pants may fall down.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.854
That's why I have a
belt and suspenders.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.222
I'm used to building big stuff.

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:38.825
Some of my sculptures
weigh up to 6,500 pounds.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:43.129
So this is in the realm of
what I'm used to working on.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.666
My quench tank's buried
six feet in the Earth.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.835
Here we go up this ladder.

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:51.970
This thing is huge.

00:02:52.037 --> 00:02:53.072
Guard, done.

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.740
Pommel's done.

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:59.312
The plan is to grind a pound
and a half up this blade.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.813
Everything's coming out.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:02.581
Nice shape.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:05.418
And I'm going to start
the finish of the blade,

00:03:05.484 --> 00:03:08.921
and the guard, and the pommel.

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:11.323
I'm really happy with it.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.326
I really got to hit
something with this.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:16.962
I just can't let it pass.

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:18.330
Nasty.

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:22.635
DOUG: Bladesmiths,
welcome to the KEAL test.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.036
John, you are up first.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.170
Are you ready?

00:03:25.237 --> 00:03:26.172
- You know I'm ready.
- All right.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:27.473
Let's do this.

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:34.214
[music playing]

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:52.465
All right, John.

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.433
Let's talk about
your sword here.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.471
What I like is that you use
a nice, hefty counterbalance

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.906
because despite being a
heavy sword like this,

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:02.642
it's wieldable.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:03.775
The edges are sharp.

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.178
It cut deeply.

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:08.947
I like that the guard
also is not so massive

00:04:09.014 --> 00:04:10.483
that it gets in the way.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.385
And overall, it will KEAL.

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:15.288
- Awesome.
- All right, Michael your turn.

00:04:15.354 --> 00:04:16.788
You ready, sir?
- Yes, sir.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:17.890
Let's do this.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:23.997
[music playing]

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:46.719
All right, Michael.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:48.020
That's a beast.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.822
That's a beautiful beast.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.491
Now, your edge is sharp.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.493
That really cuts deep
into the pig carcass.

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:57.563
It is a nice, heavy
weight but controllable.

00:04:57.629 --> 00:05:00.066
And more importantly,
it will KEAL.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:01.367
Thank you, sir.

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:07.740
DAVE: Gentlemen, it's time
for our strength test,

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.942
the sheet metal chop.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:13.111
Now, there are three layers
of 20-gauge sheet metal

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:14.313
in each one of these things.

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:15.348
And, John, you're up first.

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:16.982
Are you ready for this?
- I am.

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:18.084
OK.

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:28.661
[music playing]

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.123
All right, John.

00:05:58.223 --> 00:05:59.926
Very nice job on this sword.

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:01.827
It's got a good weight to it.

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:04.429
The blade itself, and there
are some slight rolls.

00:06:04.496 --> 00:06:06.098
But it's still plenty sharp.

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:09.468
One comment I might have is
that the blades lean enough

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:10.903
that it's very flexible.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.539
When I pick it up,
that tip kind of drops

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:14.907
maybe three inches of flex.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:16.642
You start to swing that
blade, you don't know

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:17.843
where that tip's going to go.

00:06:17.943 --> 00:06:19.111
It's flopping around.

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:20.145
But it did its job.

00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:21.580
Well done.
- Thank you.

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:23.349
DAVE: All right, Michael,
are you ready for this?

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:24.617
Give it hell.

00:06:24.683 --> 00:06:25.918
DAVE: Oh, good because
this is so much fun.

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.054
[laughs]

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:38.931
[music playing]

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:08.594
All right, Michael, balance
point, right where I freaking

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:09.628
want it.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:10.997
It's a little bit
more of a firm blade.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.366
It doesn't quite wiggle as much.

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:17.503
As far as damage, just
the very edge is rolled.

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:20.907
But I would not drag my
finger down that edge.

00:07:21.007 --> 00:07:22.608
It would still open me up.

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:24.276
Knocked it out of the park.
Well done.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.578
Thank you.

00:07:29.548 --> 00:07:31.083
DOUG: All right, bladesmiths.

00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:35.454
This is a sharpness test-- the
watermelon and water jug slice.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.056
John, you're up first.
Are you ready for this?

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:37.856
- I am.
- All right.

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:38.991
Let's do this.

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:50.937
[music playing]

00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:01.113
All right, John.

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:02.548
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.450
The edge, it cut
the first watermelon

00:08:04.516 --> 00:08:06.485
cleanly and then just wobbled.

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.053
There's so much wobble in this.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.390
As Dave said, there's just
so much flex in this blade,

00:08:11.456 --> 00:08:12.891
you don't know where
the tip is going to go.

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:15.194
And that's what happened
in the watermelon slice.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.762
On the water jugs, clean cut.

00:08:16.828 --> 00:08:18.931
Even the tops are still there
wondering what happened.

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:22.702
Overall, your Grote
Pier, it will cut.

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:24.436
Thank you.
DOUG: Michael, your turn.

00:08:24.503 --> 00:08:25.404
Are you ready?
- You bet.

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:26.772
All right.
Let's do this.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.049
[music playing]

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:48.560
All right, Michael, let's
talk about your blade here.

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:51.630
First up, on a watermelon
slice, much easier.

00:08:51.696 --> 00:08:53.465
Not much flex on the blade here.

00:08:53.532 --> 00:08:54.500
It's easy to just
keep it and let

00:08:54.566 --> 00:08:55.768
it drop and let it do its work.

00:08:55.834 --> 00:08:57.569
And it cuts all the way through.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.239
On the water jug
slice, it did cut.

00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:02.908
Overall, your
sword, it will cut.

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:03.943
Thank you.

00:09:07.747 --> 00:09:10.282
That was pretty rad.

00:09:10.382 --> 00:09:11.483
All right, gentlemen.

00:09:11.583 --> 00:09:12.851
In this super-sized
competition, we

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:15.454
gave you a task on a scale
unlike anything we've

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:16.955
ever seen before in this forge.

00:09:17.022 --> 00:09:20.659
And the blades you brought
back here impressed all of us.

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:22.060
But as you know,
only one of you can

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:23.529
take home the title of
"Forged in Fire" champion

00:09:23.595 --> 00:09:24.863
and get that check for $10,000.

00:09:24.963 --> 00:09:27.066
And the judges did
make a final decision.

00:09:27.132 --> 00:09:31.503
Today's "Forged in Fire"
champion is Michael.

00:09:31.604 --> 00:09:32.771
Congratulations.

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:34.573
John, you gave us an
unbelievable blade.

00:09:34.673 --> 00:09:35.841
But it missed by just this much.

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.109
And Dave is going
to tell you why.

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:38.844
DAVE: John, this
decision really came down

00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:41.213
to the extreme
flexibility of your blade.

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:43.215
That's why we're letting you go.

00:09:43.316 --> 00:09:45.351
GRADY POWELL: John, you
are an unbelievable smith.

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:47.886
But unfortunately, your time
in this competition has ended.

00:09:47.953 --> 00:09:48.821
I'm going to have
to ask you to please

00:09:48.888 --> 00:09:49.855
step off the forge floor.

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:51.023
I had a blast.
Thank you, guys.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.091
Thank you, John.

00:09:52.157 --> 00:09:53.359
GRADY POWELL: Well,
Michael, that makes you

00:09:53.425 --> 00:09:54.626
the "Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:54.693 --> 00:09:56.228
You're taking home
a check for $10,000.

00:09:56.295 --> 00:09:57.229
Well done, man.

00:09:57.329 --> 00:09:58.463
Woo.

00:09:58.563 --> 00:10:01.267
I'm "Forged in Fire" champion.

00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:05.271
This is the first blade I've
ever forged of this size.

00:10:05.338 --> 00:10:06.905
I'm very proud of
my performance.

00:10:07.005 --> 00:10:09.107
Very important to me
to share the craft,

00:10:09.208 --> 00:10:13.245
and to educate people, and
make some badass swords.

00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:16.382
[music playing]

